 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Family",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "NE5532, NE5532A, SA5532 and SA5532A are dual amplifiers with low noise, strong output drive, wide unity-gain and full-swing bandwidth, low distortion and fast slewing. Diodes protect the inputs and the outputs survive short circuits. Each amplifier is compensated on chip for unity gain, and input noise voltage carries a guaranteed maximum. The NE parts cover 0 °C to 70 °C and the SA parts -40 °C to 85 °C; the SO (PS) package is offered for the NE parts only."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Inside each amplifier",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "A bipolar differential input pair fed by a current source drives a current-mirror load; the signal then passes through a resistor to a pre-driver compensated by capacitor CC, which drives a complementary bipolar output pair with emitter resistors between VCC+ and VCC–."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bandwidth, CMRR and slew rate",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Unity-gain bandwidth is the highest frequency at which a gain-of-one stage still passes a signal without heavy distortion. CMRR is the change in input offset voltage per change in common-mode input, in decibels; it is 100 dB typical. Slew rate is the fastest rate the output can change after an input step."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "The amplifiers are specified from ±5 V to ±15 V, and many limits hold over 0 °C to 70 °C (NE) or -40 °C to 85 °C (SA). They are on whenever the supply is present and work from either a single supply or split supplies. The absolute maximum supply is +18 V on VCC+ and -18 V on VCC–. Supplies beyond ±22 V can damage the part permanently."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input current",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "The input voltage must not exceed the supply voltage in magnitude. More than about 0.6 V across the inputs drives heavy input current unless a series resistor limits it; the limit is ±10 mA."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply bypass",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "A 0.1 µF bypass capacitor next to the supply pins stops noise from a noisy or high-impedance supply getting in. With split supplies each supply pin gets its own low-ESR 0.1 µF ceramic to ground, as close to the part as it can go; with one supply, a single capacitor from V+ to ground does."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Keep analog and digital grounds separate and mind the path of ground current; a ground plane on an inner layer spreads heat and reduces EMI pickup. Input traces should be short and kept away from supply and output traces, crossing a noisy trace at right angles when they must cross. External parts go close to the amplifier, with RF and RG right beside the inverting input to keep its stray capacitance low. A guard ring driven at low impedance around critical traces cuts leakage from nearby traces at other voltages."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Typical application: parts choice",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "In the single-ended to differential converter the input common-mode range and output swing set the DC linearity, so the part wanted is one whose inputs and output both reach the rails. VOUT– depends directly on R1 to R4, so close-tolerance resistors keep its error low."
  }
 ],
